# core.autocrlf=true on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF in anything Git guesses is
# text. Some QA assets fool that guess — a few element_*.png are actually SVG
# markup saved under a .png name, and Git happily "converted" them. A single
# byte changed inside a golden makes Paparazzi report a visual difference that
# does not exist, and the tester chases a phantom regression. Mark every binary
# asset explicitly so no line-ending rewriting can ever touch it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.svg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.aar binary
*.jar binary

# Text assets: normalise to LF in the repo, so the same file committed from
# Windows and from macOS/Linux is byte-identical.
*.kt      text eol=lf
*.kts     text eol=lf
*.java    text eol=lf
*.xml     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.pro     text eol=lf
gradlew   text eol=lf
*.bat     text eol=crlf
